---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Nav from "../components/Nav.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
---

<!--
  A private share for one client, not part of the portfolio. It carries noindex
  and is filtered out of the sitemap in astro.config.mjs, so the two agree.
-->
<Layout
  title="Ohio Golf Club — Website Review"
  description="A review of ohiogolfclubindoor.com and how the club shows up in Google and AI assistants."
  noindex={true}
>
  <Nav />
  <main class="review">
    <header class="masthead">
      <p class="stamp">3 September 2026 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Ryan Sheridan</p>
      <h1>Ohio Golf Club: what&rsquo;s working, and what could work better</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        I looked at <a href="https://www.ohiogolfclubindoor.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ohiogolfclubindoor.com</a> and at how the club shows up in Google and AI assistants. Your reputation and your pro are
        doing the work, and doing it well. This is about getting the website to carry more of that load.
      </p>
      <p class="lede">
        Every claim has a link or a screenshot. Where I couldn&rsquo;t verify something, I&rsquo;ve said so.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <div class="sec-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Summary</p>
        <h2>The short version</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="box">
        <p>
          <strong>Your information isn&rsquo;t all in the right places.</strong> Some facts aren&rsquo;t on the
          site at all, like your hours. Others are on the page but not written into the code in the
          format Google and AI assistants look for, so they fall back to directory sites. Those
          directories then repeat details that are out of date, including a price you don&rsquo;t charge
          and an address that isn&rsquo;t yours.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>You only have one page really competing.</strong> Lessons, fittings, memberships and
          leagues are all sections of your homepage. Giving each its own page creates more ways to be
          found, and lands people on the exact thing they searched for.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="sec-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Strengths</p>
        <h2>What you&rsquo;re already doing well</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="wins">
        <div class="win">
          <span class="m" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <h4>You&rsquo;re the top answer for the questions that matter</h4>
            <p>
              Tested 2 September: asking an AI assistant where to take indoor golf lessons near Akron,
              and where to get a serious TrackMan fitting, returned Ohio Golf Club first both times,
              ahead of GOLFTEC Fairlawn and Golf Galaxy.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="win">
          <span class="m" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <h4>4.9 stars, with listings showing 85 to 87 reviews</h4>
            <p>Your strongest marketing asset.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="win">
          <span class="m" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <h4>Your <a href="https://business.google.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Business Profile</a> is doing real work</h4>
            <p>
              Rating, category, photos and open-now status all show up in map results. Whoever set it
              up did a good job.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="win">
          <span class="m" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <h4>The site is built on current technology</h4>
            <p>
              It runs on <a href="https://nextjs.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Next.js</a>, a
              modern framework, not an aging template. That&rsquo;s a good foundation, and it makes
              everything below straightforward to add.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="win">
          <span class="m" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <h4>Booking is properly connected</h4>
            <p>
              Bays, lessons and memberships all run through <a href="https://clubcaddie.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Club Caddie</a> rather
              than being pieced together.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="win">
          <span class="m" aria-hidden="true">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <h4>Your credentials are genuinely differentiating</h4>
            <p>
              GOLFTEC certified, Titleist and Callaway master fitter, 7,500 lessons, 500 fittings.
              Golf Galaxy can&rsquo;t say that. It just isn&rsquo;t working as hard for you as it could.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="sec-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Findings, tagged by priority</p>
        <h2>What could work better</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">

        <article class="item now">
          <span class="badge now">Costing you now</span>
          <h3><span class="n">01</span>Your hours aren&rsquo;t on your website</h3>
          <div class="b">
            <div>
              <p>
                They&rsquo;re not on the <a href="https://www.ohiogolfclubindoor.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">homepage</a>, the <a href="https://www.ohiogolfclubindoor.com/contact-us" target="_blank" rel="noopener">contact page</a>,
                or anywhere else. Three directories publish hours for you instead, and no two agree.
              </p>
              <div class="tw">
                <table>
                  <thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>Hours it shows</th></tr></thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr><td class="n">Your website</td><td class="bad">Nothing listed</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="n"><a href="https://golfsimspot.com/golf-simulator-business/ohio-golf-club/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Golf Sim Spot</a></td><td>Mon to Fri 9am to 8pm, weekends 9am to 5pm</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="n"><a href="https://teebly.io/venue/a290afbe-c14a-4e82-9706-c9399d45dd3a" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Teebly</a></td><td>Daily 8am to 10pm</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="n"><a href="https://screengolfers.com/usa/ohio/akron/ohio-golf-club-44333" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Screengolfers</a></td><td>Mon to Sat 8am to 10pm, Sun 8am to 8pm</td></tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
            <figure>
              <p class="k">Asked for your hours, an AI assistant didn&rsquo;t read your site. It answered from a directory called GolfSimMap and said so.</p>
              <img
                src="/ohio-golf-club/screenshot-1.jpg"
                alt="Screenshot of an AI assistant listing Ohio Golf Club's hours and citing GolfSimMap as the source."
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </figure>
            <p>
              Someone deciding whether to drive over right now can&rsquo;t find out from you. Everyone
              answering that question on your behalf is reading someone else&rsquo;s page.
            </p>
            <div class="opts two">
              <div class="opt rec">
                <div class="top"><h4>Publish and correct everywhere</h4><span class="tag b">Recommended</span></div>
                <p>
                  Hours on the site, written into the page code in the format search engines look for,
                  then your Google profile and the three directories updated to match.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="opt">
                <div class="top"><h4>Publish on the site only</h4><span class="tag">Partial</span></div>
                <p>Visitors can see them. Search engines and AI assistants keep using the directory versions.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="item up">
          <span class="badge up">Biggest upside</span>
          <h3><span class="n">02</span>Ask an AI your prices twice and you get two different answers</h3>
          <div class="b">
            <figure>
              <p class="k">Asked one way, it listed every tier correctly, read from your own website.</p>
              <img
                src="/ohio-golf-club/screenshot-2.jpg"
                alt="Screenshot of an AI assistant correctly listing all four Ohio Golf Club membership tiers and prices."
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </figure>
            <figure>
              <p class="k">Asked another way the same day, it said your pricing &ldquo;is not published clearly online&rdquo; and offered to go hunting.</p>
              <img
                src="/ohio-golf-club/screenshot-3.jpg"
                alt="Screenshot of an AI assistant saying Ohio Golf Club's membership pricing is not published clearly online."
                loading="lazy"
              />
            </figure>
            <p>
              When Google or ChatGPT can&rsquo;t confidently read your page, they go looking elsewhere and
              land on a directory. That&rsquo;s how the wrong numbers spread, and you have no say in which
              version someone gets.
            </p>
            <div>
              <p class="k">The fix</p>
              <p>
                A standard format, published at <a href="https://schema.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">schema.org</a>, for spelling
                the same facts out in your page&rsquo;s code. Google, Bing and the AI assistants all read
                it. Visitors never see it.
              </p>
              <div class="codes">
                <div>
                  <p class="k">On the page today</p>
                  <pre class="code">&lt;h3&gt;Classic&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;$200/Month&lt;/p&gt;</pre>
                </div>
                <div>
                  <p class="k">Added alongside it</p>
                  <pre class="code">&lbrace;
  "@type": "Offer",
  "name": "Classic Membership",
  "price": "200.00",
  "priceCurrency": "USD"
&rbrace;</pre>
                </div>
              </div>
              <p class="cap">
                Left, Google has to guess which line is the membership name and which is the price.
                Right, it&rsquo;s spelled out and there&rsquo;s nothing to guess. <em>Simplified example, not your actual code.</em>
              </p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="k">Both put the same facts in the code. The difference is where that code comes from.</p>
              <div class="opts two">
                <div class="opt rec">
                  <div class="top"><h4>One place to edit</h4><span class="tag b">Recommended</span></div>
                  <p>Built from your page content, so there&rsquo;s one source. Change a price once and both update.</p>
                </div>
                <div class="opt">
                  <div class="top"><h4>Two places to edit</h4><span class="tag">Partial</span></div>
                  <p>Written separately, so every price change has to be made twice. Miss one and they disagree.</p>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="item up">
          <span class="badge up">Quick win</span>
          <h3><span class="n">03</span>All five pages carry the same hidden title</h3>
          <div class="b">
            <p>
              Every page has a title and description written into its code. You don&rsquo;t see them on the
              page. They&rsquo;re what Google shows as the clickable blue headline in search results, and
              what appears on your browser tab.
            </p>
            <div>
              <p>Your visible headings are all different and fine. It&rsquo;s the hidden ones underneath that are identical across all five:</p>
              <ul>
                <li><a href="https://www.ohiogolfclubindoor.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Homepage</a></li>
                <li><a href="https://www.ohiogolfclubindoor.com/team" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Our Team</a></li>
                <li><a href="https://www.ohiogolfclubindoor.com/contact-us" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Contact Us</a></li>
                <li><a href="https://www.ohiogolfclubindoor.com/events" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Events &amp; Bar</a></li>
                <li><a href="https://www.ohiogolfclubindoor.com/carls-place" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Carl&rsquo;s Place</a>, your custom simulator installation page</li>
              </ul>
              <p class="after-list">All five say &ldquo;Ohio Golf Club | Premier Indoor Golf Experience&rdquo; with the same description underneath.</p>
            </div>
            <p>
              Google reads that as five copies of one page, so only your homepage really competes.
              Carl&rsquo;s Place is the clearest example: a separate business line selling home simulator
              installations, and Google can&rsquo;t tell it apart from your contact page.
            </p>
            <p><strong>The fix:</strong> each page gets its own title and description. No second option worth presenting, and the cheapest item here.</p>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="item up">
          <span class="badge up">Biggest upside</span>
          <h3><span class="n">04</span>Your services don&rsquo;t have pages of their own</h3>
          <div class="b">
            <p>
              Lessons, fittings, memberships and bay rentals are sections of the homepage you reach by
              scrolling. Someone searching for golf lessons in Akron has no page of yours to land on. <a href="https://cornerclubgolf.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Corner Club</a> already runs separate pages for rates, technology, lessons and leagues.
            </p>
            <figure>
              <p class="k">Today every search funnels to one page. With service pages, each search lands on the thing it asked for.</p>
              <div class="dw">
              <svg
                viewBox="0 0 470 372"
                role="img"
                aria-label="Diagram comparing today, where several searches funnel into the homepage, with a proposed structure where each search lands on its own service page with its own booking action."
                class="diagram"
              >
                <defs>
                  <marker id="ar" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><polygon points="0,1 8,4 0,7" fill="#7a7a7a"></polygon></marker>
                  <marker id="arb" viewBox="0 0 8 8" refX="7" refY="4" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto"><polygon points="0,1 8,4 0,7" fill="#111111"></polygon></marker>
                </defs>
                <g>
                  <text x="0" y="12" font-size="13" font-weight="500" fill="#111111">Today</text>
                  <g font-size="11.5" fill="#555555" text-anchor="end">
                    <text x="150" y="42">&ldquo;golf lessons akron&rdquo;</text>
                    <text x="150" y="66">&ldquo;club fitting akron&rdquo;</text>
                    <text x="150" y="90">&ldquo;indoor golf akron&rdquo;</text>
                  </g>
                  <g stroke="#7a7a7a" stroke-width="1" fill="none">
                    <path d="M156 38 L184 62"></path>
                    <path d="M156 62 L184 62"></path>
                    <path d="M156 86 L184 62"></path>
                  </g>
                  <line x1="184" y1="62" x2="205" y2="62" stroke="#7a7a7a" stroke-width="1" marker-end="url(#ar)"></line>
                  <rect x="212" y="45" width="140" height="34" fill="#ffffff" stroke="#cfcfcf" stroke-width="1"></rect>
                  <text x="282" y="66" font-size="12.5" text-anchor="middle" fill="#111111">Homepage</text>
                  <text x="470" y="66" font-size="10.5" fill="#7a7a7a" text-anchor="end">everything lives here</text>
                  <line x1="0" y1="120" x2="470" y2="120" stroke="#e5e5e5" stroke-width="1"></line>
                  <text x="0" y="148" font-size="13" font-weight="500" fill="#111111">With service pages</text>
                  <g font-size="11.5" fill="#555555" text-anchor="end">
                    <text x="150" y="182">&ldquo;golf lessons akron&rdquo;</text>
                    <text x="150" y="220">&ldquo;club fitting akron&rdquo;</text>
                    <text x="150" y="258">&ldquo;golf membership akron&rdquo;</text>
                    <text x="150" y="296">&ldquo;simulator rental akron&rdquo;</text>
                    <text x="150" y="334">&ldquo;indoor golf league akron&rdquo;</text>
                  </g>
                  <g stroke="#111111" stroke-width="1">
                    <line x1="158" y1="178" x2="205" y2="178" marker-end="url(#arb)"></line>
                    <line x1="158" y1="216" x2="205" y2="216" marker-end="url(#arb)"></line>
                    <line x1="158" y1="254" x2="205" y2="254" marker-end="url(#arb)"></line>
                    <line x1="158" y1="292" x2="205" y2="292" marker-end="url(#arb)"></line>
                    <line x1="158" y1="330" x2="205" y2="330" marker-end="url(#arb)"></line>
                  </g>
                  <g fill="#f9f9f9" stroke="#111111" stroke-width="1">
                    <rect x="212" y="165" width="148" height="26"></rect>
                    <rect x="212" y="203" width="148" height="26"></rect>
                    <rect x="212" y="241" width="148" height="26"></rect>
                    <rect x="212" y="279" width="148" height="26"></rect>
                    <rect x="212" y="317" width="148" height="26"></rect>
                  </g>
                  <g font-size="12" fill="#111111">
                    <text x="222" y="182">Lessons</text>
                    <text x="222" y="220">Club fitting</text>
                    <text x="222" y="258">Memberships</text>
                    <text x="222" y="296">Bay rentals</text>
                    <text x="222" y="334">Leagues</text>
                  </g>
                  <g font-size="10.5" fill="#555555">
                    <text x="370" y="182">Book</text>
                    <text x="370" y="220">Book</text>
                    <text x="370" y="258">Join</text>
                    <text x="370" y="296">Book</text>
                    <text x="370" y="334">Sign up</text>
                  </g>
                </g>
              </svg>
              </div>
            </figure>
            <div>
              <p class="k">Why this is the biggest one</p>
              <ul>
                <li><strong>More doors.</strong> Each page can rank for its own searches. A homepage section can&rsquo;t.</li>
                <li><strong>A shorter path to booking.</strong> Someone searching for club fitting lands on a fitting page with a fitting booking button, instead of your homepage where they have to scroll and work it out.</li>
                <li><strong>A link you can send.</strong> One URL for lessons that works in an ad, an Instagram bio, or a text.</li>
                <li><strong>Room to sell.</strong> A fitting page can explain why yours beats a big box store. That case can&rsquo;t be made in a homepage paragraph.</li>
                <li><strong>You learn what people want.</strong> You&rsquo;d see whether fittings or memberships draw more interest, instead of guessing.</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="opts two">
              <div class="opt rec">
                <div class="top"><h4>Five pages</h4><span class="tag b">Recommended</span></div>
                <p>Lessons, club fitting, memberships, bay rentals, leagues. Everything you sell.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="opt">
                <div class="top"><h4>Three pages</h4><span class="tag">Smaller start</span></div>
                <p>Lessons, fitting and memberships first, the rest added later.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="item now">
          <span class="badge now">Costing you now</span>
          <h3><span class="n">05</span>Other sites list the wrong address and price</h3>
          <div class="b">
            <div>
              <p>All three are live right now, and none are under your control.</p>
              <div class="tw">
                <table>
                  <thead><tr><th>Site</th><th>What it says</th><th>Actually</th></tr></thead>
                  <tbody>
                    <tr><td class="n"><a href="https://screengolfers.com/usa/ohio/akron/ohio-golf-club-44333" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Screengolfers</a></td><td class="bad">215 Springside Dr, 44333</td><td>2262 S Arlington Rd, 44319</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="n"><a href="https://golfsimspot.com/golf-simulator-business/ohio-golf-club/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Golf Sim Spot</a></td><td class="bad">Membership $250 a month</td><td>Classic is $200 a month</td></tr>
                    <tr><td class="n"><a href="https://teebly.io/venue/a290afbe-c14a-4e82-9706-c9399d45dd3a" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Teebly</a></td><td>Daily 8am to 10pm</td><td>Conflicts with both others</td></tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p>
              The wrong address is almost certainly left over from the move. Directories don&rsquo;t update
              themselves when a business relocates, so old details sit there until someone corrects
              them. Not anyone&rsquo;s mistake, but the longer it sits the more sites copy it.
            </p>
            <p>
              Someone following the Screengolfers address drives to a place that isn&rsquo;t you. Someone
              comparing prices who sees $250 may rule you out before ever reaching your site.
            </p>
            <div class="opts two">
              <div class="opt rec">
                <div class="top"><h4>Full sweep</h4><span class="tag b">Recommended</span></div>
                <p>
                  Correct these three, then the rest, so your name, address, phone and hours match
                  everywhere. Consistency across listings is one of the things Google uses to rank
                  local businesses.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="opt">
                <div class="top"><h4>The main three</h4><span class="tag">Smaller start</span></div>
                <p>Just the listings appearing in searches and AI answers today.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="item">
          <span class="badge">Worth doing</span>
          <h3><span class="n">06</span>Your 4.9 rating isn&rsquo;t on your own site</h3>
          <div class="b">
            <p>
              There are member quotes on the homepage, but no rating, no review count, and no way for a
              member to leave one. It&rsquo;s your strongest proof, missing at the exact moment someone
              decides whether to book.
            </p>
            <div class="opts two">
              <div class="opt rec">
                <div class="top"><h4>Show it as text</h4><span class="tag b">Recommended</span></div>
                <p>
                  Display the rating and count, link to your Google reviews, refresh occasionally. No
                  cost, nothing to break, and the number moves slowly enough to stay accurate.
                </p>
              </div>
              <div class="opt">
                <div class="top"><h4>Pull it automatically</h4><span class="tag">More moving parts</span></div>
                <p>
                  Connect to Google so it updates itself. Small ongoing cost, limits on how many reviews
                  can be pulled, and rules on display. A lot of machinery for a number that barely changes.
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="k">What this can and can&rsquo;t do</p>
              <p>
                <strong>You can</strong> show your rating, your review count and your reviews on your own
                website, and link people to your Google listing to read more or leave one.
              </p>
              <p class="spaced">
                <strong>You can&rsquo;t</strong> make star ratings appear under your listing in Google search
                results this way. <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/structured-data/review-snippet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google&rsquo;s own documentation</a> says a business that controls the reviews about itself is &ldquo;ineligible for star review
                feature&rdquo;, and separately, &ldquo;don&rsquo;t aggregate reviews or ratings from other
                websites&rdquo;. If anyone offers you stars in search results from this, they&rsquo;re overselling.
              </p>
              <p class="spaced">
                <strong>Still worth doing,</strong> because it&rsquo;s the proof people look for in the seconds
                before they book, and right now it isn&rsquo;t there.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="item now">
          <span class="badge now">Time sensitive</span>
          <h3><span class="n">07</span>No league page, and league season is starting</h3>
          <div class="b">
            <p>Your site&rsquo;s own description advertises indoor leagues. There&rsquo;s no page about them.</p>
            <p>
              League signups are a deadline, not something people browse for, and a league fills your
              quietest hours with groups who come back weekly. With outdoor season winding down and new
              pages taking weeks to months to gain traction in search, a page built now has a shot at
              this season. One built in December doesn&rsquo;t.
            </p>
            <div class="opts two">
              <div class="opt rec">
                <div class="top"><h4>Simple page now</h4><span class="tag b">Recommended</span></div>
                <p>Format, schedule, cost and how to join, live in time for signups.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="opt">
                <div class="top"><h4>Page plus online signup</h4><span class="tag">Fuller build</span></div>
                <p>Adds registration. Better long term, likely misses this season.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </article>

        <article class="item">
          <span class="badge">Worth doing</span>
          <h3><span class="n">08</span>Form submissions go to your inbox and nowhere else</h3>
          <div class="b">
            <p>
              The <a href="https://www.ohiogolfclubindoor.com/contact-us" target="_blank" rel="noopener">contact form</a> collects name, phone, email, interest and a message. You mentioned those land in your
              email and go no further, so there&rsquo;s no list you can work from, and no way to see who
              asked about lessons three months ago and never booked.
            </p>
            <div class="opts">
              <div class="opt rec">
                <div class="top"><h4>Use what you already pay for</h4><span class="tag b">Check this first</span></div>
                <p>
                  <a href="https://clubcaddie.com/solutions/customer-management/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Club Caddie&rsquo;s customer management</a> already does customer profiles, purchase and playing history, grouping by member type,
